.TH KP-SYNC 1 "2026-11-04" "kp-sync 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kp-sync \- synchronize KeePass copies and manage access sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kp-sync
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\-profile\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-ui\fR \fIMODE\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-wait\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-daemon\fR]
[\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-debug\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fICOMMAND-OPTIONS\fR] [\fIARGS\fR ...]
.br
.B kp-sync \-\-version
.br
.B kp-sync
\fICOMMAND\fR \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kp-sync
keeps one KeePass database consistent across several copies: a local
.I hub
file that applications open, cloud remotes reached through
.BR rclone (1),
removable drives, fixed files, custom commands and temporary remote sessions.
Each copy is compared with a shared version history by content hash, not by
modification time. A single changed copy is promoted to the others; several
changed copies are merged field by field against their common ancestor.
Every candidate is verified before it is written, and every overwrite is
preceded by a backup.
.PP
Most commands are short clients of a per-profile
.I daemon
that runs only while something needs it (an application, a drive, a session
or a pending decision) and is started on demand through a systemd user
socket. The daemon serializes synchronizations and asks the user questions
on the terminal or desktop.
.B \-\-no\-daemon
runs the same engine directly in the current process where supported.
.PP
Configuration is read from
.I ~/.config/kp-sync/config.toml
by default; see
.BR kp-sync-config (5)
for every setting. A
.I profile
is an independent configuration with its own hub, runtime directory,
daemon and password cache, selected with
.BR \-\-profile .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options are written before the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read configuration from
.I FILE
instead of the default file. The file must exist. Runtime state still
belongs to the default profile; use
.B \-\-profile
for an independent instance. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-profile .
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Use the named profile
.IR ~/.config/kp-sync/NAME.toml ,
which must exist.
.I NAME
starts with a letter or digit and contains only letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- .
A named profile has its own runtime directory
.RI ( $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kp-sync-NAME/ ),
default hub
.RI ( $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/NAME/pass.kdbx ),
daemon instance, control socket and password keyring entry. Without this
option (and without
.BR \-\-config ),
the default profile is used.
.TP
.BI \-\-ui " MODE"
How to ask the user questions (merge decisions, passwords, conflict
review, confirmations). Overrides
.B [ui].mode
for this command.
.RS
.TP
.B auto
Use the terminal when standard input is a terminal, otherwise the desktop
when
.B DISPLAY
or
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set, otherwise
.BR none .
This is the default.
.TP
.B tty
Ask on the terminal of the waiting client.
.TP
.B gui
Ask with desktop dialogs chosen by
.BR [ui].gui ,
shown by the daemon.
.TP
.B none
Never ask. Every question takes its safe answer: nothing is overwritten,
the decision is recorded as pending, and the command exits with status 3.
Use
.B kp-sync resolve
later to answer it.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
For commands that send a request to the daemon, return as soon as the
daemon has accepted it instead of waiting for the synchronization result.
The exit status then only reports acceptance. Also accepted after the
command name for
.BR sync ,
.BR resolve ,
.BR run ,
.BR eject ,
.BR up ,
.BR down ,
.B attach
and
.BR detach .
.BR up ,
.BR down ,
.B attach
and
.B detach
never wait, with or without this option.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-daemon
Run the synchronization engine in this process under a file lock instead
of contacting the daemon. Supported by
.BR sync ,
.B resolve
and
.BR restore .
The lock wait is limited by
.BR [sync].lock_timeout .
Useful for scripts, debugging and machines without the systemd units. Also
accepted after the command name for
.B sync
and
.BR resolve .
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Accepted for compatibility. Informational
.B INFO:
messages are currently always written to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Accepted for compatibility; currently adds no output.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the package version and exit without loading configuration or
contacting the daemon or any endpoint.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of options. After a command name, show that command's
options.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "Applications and synchronization"
.TP
\fBrun\fR [\fB\-\-app\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-raise\-existing\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-wait\fR] [[\fB\-\-\fR] \fIARGS\fR ...]
Synchronize the hub, start an application on it and hold a
.I lease
for as long as the application runs, so the daemon syncs each save and
performs a final
.I post-sync
when it exits. This is the command behind the KeePassXC menu entry,
autostart and launcher scripts.
.RS
.PP
Before launch the daemon
.I pre-syncs
for up to
.B [daemon].prestart_timeout
seconds. If that takes longer and a hub exists, the application starts on
the existing hub and the user is notified (or asked, with
.BR [launch].confirm_without_cloud ).
If no hub exists yet, the launch waits for the first download to finish.
If no copy is reachable at all, the application is not started, so it is
never left to create an empty database. Each application runs in its own
transient systemd user unit
.RB ( kp-sync-app-NAME ),
so closing the terminal does not end it.
.TP
.BI \-\-app " NAME"
Application to start, from the built-in
.B keepassxc
and
.B keepass-legacy
definitions or a configured
.B [apps.NAME]
table. Default:
.BR [launch].default_app .
.TP
.B \-\-raise\-existing
When a single-instance application such as KeePassXC is already running,
activate its window even though standard input is not a terminal (for
example from a desktop launcher). An already-running single instance is
activated without a pre-sync; a new instance is still started by the
daemon.
.TP
.I ARGS
Extra arguments passed to the application after its configured command
(or to the running instance of a single-instance application). A leading
.B \-\-
separates them from kp-sync options and is removed. Without
.IR ARGS ,
the built-in
.B keepassxc
and
.B keepass-legacy
applications are given the hub path.
.RE
.TP
\fBsync\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-daemon\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-wait\fR]
Probe every configured and attached copy, classify each one against the
history, and promote or merge as needed. Prints the outcome, the current
version and the state of each copy:
.RS
.TP
.B unchanged
Holds the current version (or a plain re-save of it).
.TP
.B stale
Holds an older known version; it is overwritten with the current one
without needing the password.
.TP
.B changed
Holds new content derived from a known version; it is promoted, or merged
when other copies changed too.
.TP
.B diverged
Holds a known version on another branch of the history; it is merged with
the current version, never overwritten.
.TP
.B new
Holds content with no history record (for example an old drive). It is
never promoted: it is overwritten only if its content is already contained
in the current version, otherwise merged without undoing deletions.
.TP
.B missing
Could not be reached. It is listed as
.I unreachable
and retried on the next run; nothing is queued for it.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Probe and classify copies with fresh endpoint contact, but write no copy
and no shared history. Use this to see what a real sync would do.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-daemon
As the global option: run in this process.
.RE
.TP
\fBresolve\fR [\fB\-\-no\-daemon\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-wait\fR]
Retry the decisions recorded as pending (merges that need a password or a
conflict review, unknown copies, large entry drops, ...), asking on this
terminal or desktop according to
.BR \-\-ui .
The daemon also retries them on its own whenever a display is available.
.TP
\fBrestore\fR [\fB\-\-merge\fR | \fB\-\-replace\fR] (\fB\-\-version\fR \fISHA\fR | \fB\-\-backup\fR \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR) [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Bring back older content and commit the result as a new version, so no
copy treats it as stale. Putting an old file back by hand does not work:
the next sync overwrites it or takes only its newer entries. Restoring
needs the password, shows what will change and asks for confirmation; the
large-drop check of
.B [verify]
applies. Restore is deferred if a reachable copy has unsynchronized
changes; run
.B kp-sync sync
first. Exactly one source is required:
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-version " SHA"
A version still kept in the history, by its full SHA-256 as shown by
.B kp-sync history
or
.BR "kp-sync status \-\-long" .
Abbreviated hashes are not accepted.
.TP
.BI \-\-backup " NAME"
A runtime backup file name, as listed by
.BR "kp-sync history" .
Runtime backups exist only for the current boot.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " PATH"
Any KDBX file, for example a revision downloaded by hand from the cloud
provider's version history or a rescue file from a drive.
.PP
The mode selects how it is applied:
.TP
.B \-\-merge
(default) Open the conflict review with entries missing from the current
database (including deleted ones) and entries that differ; the user picks
what to bring back, field by field. Nothing is removed from the current
database. Recovered entries get a new modification time.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Make the selected content the current database everywhere. Entries and
groups are given new modification times and later deletions are dropped
from its deletion records, so copies holding newer content cannot win a
later merge.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would change without writing anything.
.RE
.TP
.B history
List the versions retained in the history (date, host, parents, and the
entry count when the password is cached) and the runtime backups of the
current boot. It may contact the configured baseline stores to load
shared history.
.TP
\fBstatus\fR [\fB\-\-long\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show the cached state: one block per persistent copy (cloud, drive, hub,
file) and per session, with recorded version, identity and the time and
age of its last synchronization; then pending decisions and leases.
Status never probes endpoints: it shows what the last synchronization
recorded, not whether a copy is reachable now. Use
.B sync \-\-dry\-run
for fresh observations. When the daemon is running it is asked for its
live state; otherwise the runtime directory is read.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-long
Show full SHA-256 hashes. By default hashes are shortened to 12 hexadecimal
characters, or more where needed to tell recorded versions apart. Short
hashes are for display only.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object with full hashes and machine-readable identities,
for scripts.
.PP
Exits with status 10 when the recorded state says a synchronization would
change something.
.RE
.TP
.B forget
Revoke this profile's cached passwords from the kernel keyring: the main
password and any alternate-copy passwords. Does not disable a configured
KeePassXC credential source or remove its pairing.
.TP
.B keepassxc\-disconnect
Remove this host and profile's local KeePassXC browser-protocol pairing,
without contacting KeePassXC. Also revoke the connection in KeePassXC to
remove the authorization there. A later interactive credential request may
ask to pair again.
.SS "Removable drives"
A drive is identified by
.IR SERIAL:FS_UUID ,
its hardware serial number plus filesystem UUID. The mount path is ignored
for identity, since all drives may mount at the same place. When
.B \-\-drive\-id
is omitted, the identity is looked up with
.BR lsblk (8)
from the mount point. A drive is used through the configured
.B source = "runtime"
file endpoint; its database is
.IR MOUNTPOINT/[drives].directory/db_name .
These commands return once the daemon has accepted the request.
.TP
\fBattach\fR [\fB\-\-drive\-id\fR \fISERIAL:FS_UUID\fR] \fIMOUNTPOINT\fR
Register a mounted drive, take a lease for it and synchronize the hub, the
cloud and the drive. The drive stays current while attached, so it can be
removed at any moment. Used on machines whose drive is a secondary copy.
.TP
\fBdetach\fR [\fB\-\-drive\-id\fR \fIID\fR] [\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR]
Release a drive's lease, usually after it has already been removed, and
warn if it missed a synchronization that was due.
.TP
\fBup\fR [\fB\-\-drive\-id\fR \fIID\fR] [\fB\-\-app\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR]
.B attach
the drive when a mount point is given, then
.B run
the default (or selected) application. Idempotent: a repeated event does
not start a second application. A drive-triggered
.B up
synchronizes even when the application is already running. Used on the
main laptop, where inserting the key drive starts the password manager.
.TP
\fBdown\fR [\fB\-\-drive\-id\fR \fIID\fR] [\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR]
.B detach
the drive. When no drive remains and
.B [launch].quit_on_last_drive
is true, also quit the applications started by
.B up
(which runs their post-sync), then remove the runtime hub and runtime
files, including kp-sync's private rclone credential copy. A persistent
hub is never removed. A hub whose content exists on no other copy is never
deleted: it and any pending decision are kept and the user is warned.
.TP
\fBeject\fR [\fB\-\-drive\-id\fR \fIID\fR] [\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR]
Run a final synchronization including the drive, then unmount it: the
clean way to remove a drive. With
.B quit_on_last_drive
and no other drive attached, also does what
.B down
does.
.TP
\fBdrive\-sync\fR \fBup\fR|\fBdown\fR \fB\-\-mode\fR \fBmain\fR|\fBsecondary\fR \fB\-\-drive\-id\fR \fISERIAL:FS_UUID\fR [\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR]
Action for
.BR udevmon :
validate the drive identity and submit the event to the daemon without
waiting for synchronization.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-mode main
The drive holds the main copy: the event becomes
.B up
or
.BR down .
.TP
.B \-\-mode secondary
The drive is an additional copy: the event becomes
.B attach
or
.BR detach .
.PP
Both
.B \-\-mode
and
.B \-\-drive\-id
are required. An
.B up
event leaves a short-lived marker that reserves the hub until the daemon
has registered the drive, so a quick re-insertion during a slow
.B down
cannot lose data. The rclone credential file is never touched.
.RE
.SS "Remote sessions and kp-access"
.TP
\fBsession\fR [\fB\-w\fR] [\fB\-\-unconfined\fR] [\fIUSER\fR@]\fIHOST\fR[:\fIPATH\fR]
Open an interactive SSH connection to
.I HOST
and let it mount the current database with
.BR sshfs (1)
through a reverse tunnel, while the session runs in the foreground. The
current hub and key file are staged without a cloud pre-sync. A
per-session key is added to
.I ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
restricted to localhost, with an expiry
.RB ( [sessions].session_key_ttl ),
and a forced command serving only the staged directory; agent forwarding
is not used. When the session ends, remote changes are synchronized and
the key is removed.
.I PATH
is the remote mount directory (default
.BR [sessions].mount_dir ).
Requires
.BR "[sessions] enabled = true" .
.RS
.TP
.B \-w
Read-write session: remote saves are synchronized back as changes from a
foreign writer. Without it the mount is read-only, unless
.B [sessions].default_mode
is
.BR rw .
.TP
.B \-\-unconfined
Serve the SFTP session without
.BR bwrap (1)
confinement. Not recommended; use only when bwrap is unavailable.
.RE
.TP
\fBservice\fR \fBstart\fR|\fBstop\fR|\fBstatus\fR \fBssh\-session\fR|\fBaccess\-owner\fR \fINAME\fR
Manage a named SSH session
.RB ( [ssh_sessions.NAME] )
or
.B kp-access-owner
process
.RB ( [access_owners.NAME] )
as a transient systemd user service. It survives closing the terminal but
not logout or reboot unless the user manager lingers. Logs go to the user
journal. Stopping an SSH session revokes its key and completes its
post-sync. See
.B NAMED SERVICES
below.
.TP
.B access\-op
Handle one authenticated kp-access file operation (upload, delete, move)
and its BEGIN/END lease. Intended only as the
.B \-\-file\-op\-cmd
helper of
.BR kp-access ,
not for interactive use. It re-validates every path itself.
.TP
\fBsftp\-serve\fR \fIID\fR
Forced command of a session key: run
.B sftp-server
confined to that session's staging directory. Internal; not for direct
use.
.SS "Daemon and installation"
.TP
\fBdaemon\fR [\fB\-\-foreground\fR] [\fB\-\-debug\fR]
Run the per-profile daemon in the foreground. Normally started by the
systemd user service; run it by hand only for debugging, with the service
stopped. Both options are accepted for compatibility and currently change
nothing.
.TP
\fBservice\fR \fBstart\fR|\fBstop\fR|\fBrestart\fR|\fBstatus\fR \fBdaemon\fR
Manage the installed daemon unit of the default profile, or of the profile
selected by
.BR \-\-profile ,
directly through systemd. Takes no
.IR NAME .
.B start
and
.B restart
wait until the control socket answers.
.B status
never starts the daemon and also shows whether each activation unit
(socket, hub path, idle-sync timer) is active and enabled.
.B stop
leaves activation enabled, so a later trigger can start the daemon again.
A
.B \-\-config
file without a matching installed profile unit is rejected.
.TP
\fBservice\fR \fBenable\fR|\fBdisable\fR \fBdaemon\fR [\fB\-\-now\fR]
.B enable
turns on the installed activation units selected by configuration: the
control socket, the hub path unit
.RB ( [daemon].hub_path_unit )
and the idle-sync timer
.RB ( [daemon].idle_sync ).
It does not install or rewrite files.
.B disable
turns all three off, including ones no longer selected. Disabling is not
masking: explicit commands and desktop or udev launchers can still start
the daemon.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-now
Also apply the change to running units. With
.BR enable ,
start the triggers (not the daemon itself). With
.BR disable ,
stop the triggers, then any idle-sync client, then stop the daemon
gracefully. Without it, running units are left as they are.
.RE
.TP
\fBservice\fR \fBinstall\fR \fBdaemon\fR [\fB\-\-desktop\fR]
Write or refresh this profile's systemd user units and the shared
templates they need, then reload systemd. Nothing is enabled or started;
use
.B service enable daemon
afterwards. Updated files can affect units that are already active.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-desktop
Also write this profile's application menu and autostart entries, which
launch through
.BR "kp-sync run" .
.RE
.TP
\fBservice\fR \fBuninstall\fR \fBdaemon\fR [\fB\-\-desktop\fR]
Disable and stop as
.B disable \-\-now
does, then remove this profile's generated unit files and reload systemd.
Shared templates, other profiles, drop-ins, configuration, databases and
runtime recovery data are kept. Modified, unsafe or other-profile files
are refused; a failure may leave partial progress, which is reported.
Avoid issuing other requests at the same time.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-desktop
Also remove this profile's menu and autostart entries; they are kept
otherwise.
.RE
.TP
\fBdoctor\fR [\fB\-\-self\-test\fR]
Check the installation and print one
.BR OK ,
.B WARNING
or
.B ERROR
line per check: units installed and enabled, daemon socket and protocol
version, the desktop entry still calling
.BR "kp-sync run" ,
KeePassXC's automatic reload option, key file readable, rclone remotes and
probes, hub encryption when required, keyring sharing with the daemon,
swap encryption and, for sessions,
.BR bwrap ,
.BR sftp-server ,
.I authorized_keys
permissions, stale session keys and the local sshd. Some checks contact
configured endpoints. Exits 1 if any check fails.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Also authorize a five-minute restricted localhost key and perform a
confined SFTP upload and download with synthetic data. Only the test's own
key and files are removed afterwards; the database and key file are not
used. This detects shell startup output that breaks SFTP.
.RE
.SS "History maintenance"
These commands print a JSON report and change nothing unless an explicit
apply option is given. Applying to shared history requires
.BR \-\-confirm\-writers\-paused :
by giving it, you confirm that every other kp-sync writing the same history
(other hosts and other profiles) stays stopped for the whole operation.
The option does not pause them; local locks cannot protect against them on
cloud storage.
.TP
\fBcleanup\fR [\fB\-\-store\fR \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-runtime\fR] [\fB\-\-apply\fR] [\fB\-\-confirm\-writers\-paused\fR]
Preview obsolete base files (stored database versions that no retained
history refers to) in the attached baseline stores and the runtime cache,
with their size, protected files, blockers and errors. Retained versions
and the ancestry of disconnected copies are always protected; history and
database copies are never rewritten. Pending or recovery work, or
unavailable history, blocks applying. Exits 1 if the report lists errors.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-store " NAME"
Limit the operation to one configured baseline store (endpoint name from
.BR [baseline].stores ).
.TP
.B \-\-runtime
Limit the operation to the local runtime cache of bases.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Delete the previewed files. Requires
.B \-\-store
or
.BR \-\-runtime ;
with
.B \-\-store
also requires
.BR \-\-confirm\-writers\-paused .
.TP
.B \-\-confirm\-writers\-paused
See above.
.RE
.TP
\fBrepair\-history\fR [\fB\-\-apply\fR \fB\-\-confirm\-writers\-paused\fR]
Preview retained versions whose base file is missing from the available
history stores, and whether each can be recovered from another store or
cache.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Restore recoverable bases and remove versions whose base exists nowhere.
Requires
.BR \-\-confirm\-writers\-paused .
.RE
.TP
\fBretire\-session\fR [\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIID\fR] [\fB\-\-confirm\fR \fIID\fR]
Remove the records of old session copies from the history, so they no
longer appear in status or protect old versions. Database copies are not
deleted.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-identity " ID"
Preview only this exact session identity. Without any option, list every
historical session identity and whether it is eligible. The preview reads
the available shared history (it may contact baseline stores) but changes
nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-confirm " ID"
Retire the identity given with
.BR \-\-identity .
The value must match it exactly. Runtime evidence is rechecked under lock
before anything changes. Exits 3 if a shared store could not be updated;
the local record then publishes it later.
.RE
.SH NAMED SERVICES
Define SSH sessions with
.B [ssh_sessions.NAME]
tables and owner processes with
.B [access_owners.NAME]
tables in
.BR kp-sync-config (5).
For example:
.PP
.nf
[sessions]
enabled = true

[ssh_sessions.office]
target = "user@host:~/kp"
mode = "rw"

[access_owners.windows]
command = ["/path/to/venv/bin/kp-access-owner", "\-\-config", "/path/to/owner.toml"]
.fi
.PP
Then use
.PP
.nf
kp-sync service start ssh-session office
kp-sync service status ssh-session office
kp-sync service stop ssh-session office
.fi
.PP
The same commands accept
.B access-owner
as the resource type. Service commands use systemd user services and do not
require an interactive terminal. The owner command is an argv array and is
never interpreted by a shell. Shell completion offers the configured names.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Location of the default hub and of all private runtime state. Required
unless
.B hub
is configured.
.TP
.BR DISPLAY ", " WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Select desktop dialogs in
.B \-\-ui auto
when standard input is not a terminal.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Location of the KeePassXC pairing metadata, default
.IR ~/.local/state .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where KeePassXC's settings are checked for its automatic reload option,
default
.IR ~/.config .
.PP
Passwords are never read from or passed through the environment.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Install the Python package
.B argcomplete
(included as a kp-sync dependency), then add the appropriate command to the
shell startup file:
.PP
.nf
Bash: eval "$(register-python-argcomplete kp-sync)"
Zsh:  eval "$(register-python-argcomplete \-\-shell zsh kp-sync)"
Fish: register-python-argcomplete \-\-shell fish kp-sync | source
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
When several conditions apply, the first one listed among 1, 3 and 4 wins.
.TP
.B 0
Command completed successfully, all reachable copies are consistent, or a
request was accepted with
.BR \-\-no\-wait .
.TP
.B 1
Operational failure, including a daemon that cannot be reached or started;
for
.B service status
this also means the named service is inactive; for
.B doctor
a failed check.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 3
A synchronization decision is pending (see
.BR resolve ),
or shared retirement publication is unavailable or failed.
.TP
.B 4
One or more synchronization endpoints are unreachable. Endpoints marked
.B optional
do not count.
.TP
.B 10
.B status
reports that synchronization would change something.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/kp-sync/config.toml
Default configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/kp-sync/NAME.toml
Named profile configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kp-sync/
Default profile's private runtime state (mode 0700): local history copy
.RI ( state.json ),
cached bases, backups of the current boot, pending decisions, leases,
sessions, and the control socket
.IR control.sock .
Named profiles use
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kp-sync-NAME/ .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pass.kdbx
Default hub of the default profile; named profiles use
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/NAME/pass.kdbx .
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Receives one restricted, expiring line per open session, tagged
.BR kp-sync-session-PROFILE-ID ;
no other line is changed.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/kp-sync/
KeePassXC pairing metadata; never passwords or database contents.
.SH HTML DOCUMENTATION
From a source checkout, install the optional docs extra in a separate Python
environment and run
.B python tools/docs.py build
for strict HTML generation, or
.B python tools/docs.py serve
for a loopback-only preview. See docs/contributing.rst for developer checks.
Documentation builds never activate profiles or contact endpoints.
